e steps as a stageless implant, with only the C2 configuration changed."},"1754":{"id":"/docs/client/implants/creation-32","page_id":"/docs/client/implants/creation","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants/creation#relay-stageless-implant","content":"Use TCP on every supported OS. Use SMB only for Windows implants. The target host must already have a relay configured on the upstream implant. To read more about relay, see Relay."},"1755":{"id":"/docs/client/implants","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"page","content":"Implants","url":"/docs/client/implants"},"1756":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-0","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client/implants#implant-design-philosophy","content":"Implant design philosophy"},"1757":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-1","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client/implants#detection-evasion","content":"Detection evasion"},"1758":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-2","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client/implants#compatibility","content":"Compatibility"},"1759":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-3","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client/implants#features","content":"Features"},"1760":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-4","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants","content":"Implants are the binaries that run on compromised hosts. In other frameworks, they are also commonly referred to as \"beacons\"."},"1761":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-5","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants","content":"They can be created directly through the NukeRat client. To learn how to create an implant, see Creation."},"1762":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-6","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#implant-design-philosophy","content":"NukeRat implants are built around flexible customization, allowing you to create unique implants. This applies mainly to:"},"1763":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-7","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#implant-design-philosophy","content":"Implant creation: You can customize PE headers, preface code, PE strings, and other options directly from the NukeRat client."},"1764":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-8","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#implant-design-philosophy","content":"Implant API: Implant check in and task traffic use the listener implant API (encrypted HTTP POST on the channel endpointPath), not the team server management REST routes under /api/...."},"1765":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-9","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#detection-evasion","content":"NukeRat implants include several evasion techniques that are essential for modern scenarios, with indirect syscalls and sleep masking being the main ones."},"1766":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-10","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#detection-evasion","content":"Most evasion features are tied to Windows implants. Linux and macOS implants currently support string obfuscation (Off, Xor, or RC4) and PE artifact options are Windows only."},"1767":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-11","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#compatibility","content":"NukeRat implants support the following platforms:"},"1768":{"id":"/docs/client/implants-12","page_id":"/docs/client/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/client/implants#features","content":"NukeRat implants include many features essential for current scenarios. Below is the Windows implant command reference (run help on a session for the live list). Use help -os linux or help -os mac for platform specific commands."},"1769":{"id":"/docs/client","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"page","content":"Client","url":"/docs/client"},"1770":{"id":"/docs/client-0","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client","content":"NukeRat client overview"},"1771":{"id":"/docs/client-1","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"1772":{"id":"/docs/client-2","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"heading","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Topics"},"1773":{"id":"/docs/client-3","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client","content":"The NukeRat client is the operator interface for one or more team servers. After you connect and authenticate servers, use the top bar and the NukeRat menu to manage listeners, implants, and operations."},"1774":{"id":"/docs/client-4","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#getting-started","content":"Add team servers and authenticate with an operator key. See Servers."},"1775":{"id":"/docs/client-5","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#getting-started","content":"Create operator keys on the team server. See Operator key in the Server section of the docs."},"1776":{"id":"/docs/client-6","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Interface: workspace views such as terminal, folder explorer, and hosts"},"1777":{"id":"/docs/client-7","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Servers: connect and authenticate team servers"},"1778":{"id":"/docs/client-8","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Listeners: manage C2 listeners"},"1779":{"id":"/docs/client-9","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Implants: build and manage implant payloads"},"1780":{"id":"/docs/client-10","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Armory: BOF tooling in the client"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/client-11","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Pivoting: relays and network pivoting"},"1782":{"id":"/docs/client-12","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"AI agents: client AI agent features"},"1783":{"id":"/docs/client-13","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"NukeScript: automation scripts"},"1784":{"id":"/docs/client-14","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Reports: reporting"},"1785":{"id":"/docs/client-15","page_id":"/docs/client","type":"text","url":"/docs/client#topics","content":"Monitoring: monitoring views"},"1786":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"page","content":"Custom channels","url":"/docs/custom-channels"},"1787":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-0","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"1788":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-1","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-2--listener","content":"( 2 ) Listener"},"1789":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-2","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-3--custom-script","content":"( 3 ) Custom script"},"1790":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-3","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-4--third-party-platform","content":"( 4 ) Third party platform"},"1791":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-4","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-5--implant","content":"( 5 ) Implant"},"1792":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-5","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"Communication flow"},"1793":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-6","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-script","content":"Custom script"},"1794":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-7","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"Custom channel"},"1795":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-8","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#relay-and-pivoting","content":"Relay and pivoting"},"1796":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-9","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-channels#tests-upon-tests","content":"Tests upon tests"},"1797":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-10","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels","content":"If you want a communication channel through external services like Telegram, Discord, and similar, this is the page for you."},"1798":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-11","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels","content":"The NukeRat project does not influence, recommend, or promote the use of third party platforms as command and control without explicit authorization from the operator and full compliance with applicable law. Names in the list above are illustrative examples only, not endorsement or guidance by the project."},"1799":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-12","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"To build a custom channel, it helps to explain quickly how the pieces fit. It basically has three pillars:"},"1800":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-13","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"HTTP listener"},"1801":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-14","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"Implant with a custom channel"},"1802":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-15","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"Custom script"},"1803":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-16","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"Together they carry communication between the implant and the team server without the implant making any request except to the authorized third party service you chose."},"1804":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-17","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#how-it-works","content":"The flow should look like the diagram below:"},"1805":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-18","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-2--listener","content":"You need an HTTP listener configured. You can use the team server API instead, but to keep things clear and practical we will use an HTTP listener in the examples."},"1806":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-19","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-3--custom-script","content":"This is one of the most important steps. You write a script that talks to the third party service you chose and the listener, doing a kind of \"translation\"."},"1807":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-20","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-4--third-party-platform","content":"This is the platform you chose as the external communication channel."},"1808":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-21","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#-5--implant","content":"An implant that already includes custom communication logic from compile time."},"1809":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-22","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"Now that you have a sense of the structure, it is easier to walk through a scenario with an external messaging platform, like a chat similar to Telegram."},"1810":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-23","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"A whoami command is sent to the queue."},"1811":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-24","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"The custom script runs a loop that talks to the listener every 5 seconds to fetch commands. When it finds one, it sends a request to the external service, something like:"},"1812":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-25","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"On the external service, a message appears with that same content."},"1813":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-26","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"The implant already has its own requests to that external service, with the API keys and tokens needed to authenticate and send traffic. On each request it checks whether a command is waiting for it. When it finds one, it runs the command."},"1814":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-27","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"Then it sends a request to the external service with the result, something like:"},"1815":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-28","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"On one of its loops the custom script sees that output and sends a request to the listener as if it were the implant itself, following the communication format from Implant API. After that it removes that content from the external service."},"1816":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-29","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#communication-flow","content":"The listener receives the request, treats it like implant traffic, and completes the command."},"1817":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-30","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-script","content":"There is not much mystery here. You are responsible for a script that loops, watches the external service you chose, and forwards traffic to the listener. Depending on your goals you will hit more complexity here, especially around splitting very large outputs or inputs."},"1818":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-31","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"As mentioned before, the implant must be built with communication logic for the service you chose, which means writing something custom and sometimes starting from scratch."},"1819":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-32","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"To reach the custom channel during implant creation, open Command and control and select Custom channel under CHANNEL."},"1820":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-33","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"Right after that a text area appears with a code stub that looks like this:"},"1821":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-34","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"That is nothing more than the default HTTP communication written in procedural form so you can customize it more easily. You can change this logic to fit what you need."},"1822":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-35","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#custom-channel","content":"In more complex cases you may add extra helpers for fragmentation, encryption, and so on."},"1823":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-36","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#relay-and-pivoting","content":"This part is annoying to tune but necessary. Next to the Implant routine tab you will see Relay forwarder, which is the logic used by the outbound relay."},"1824":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-37","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#relay-and-pivoting","content":"When you configure a relay, there is always a last relay that sends that traffic to the team server, and that traffic must be shaped into an HTTP request and JSON. This code block is responsible for that. So if you want relay together with custom channel, you need to adjust it too."},"1825":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-38","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#relay-and-pivoting","content":"For your relief, you can often adapt the same logic from Implant routine into Relay forwarder."},"1826":{"id":"/docs/custom-channels-39","page_id":"/docs/custom-channels","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-channels#tests-upon-tests","content":"Yes, building a custom channel can be tedious and complex, so we recommend testing the full logic before you rely on it in production. For each implant build you get the exception when your logic is wrong, which makes development much easier."},"1827":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"page","content":"Custom implants","url":"/docs/custom-implants"},"1828":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-0","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-implants#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"1829":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-1","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-implants#example","content":"Example"},"1830":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-2","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/custom-implants#custom-channel","content":"Custom channel"},"1831":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-3","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-implants","content":"Sometimes you need to build an implant from scratch for something specific. When that is the case, you can use NukeRat's implant API."},"1832":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-4","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-implants#how-it-works","content":"Just to clear things up: by default, on an HTTP channel, implants talk to a REST API that the listener manages. That is the API where the implant picks up commands and sends output back."},"1833":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-5","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-implants#how-it-works","content":"Use the Implant API documentation to build the communication layer for your custom implant."},"1834":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-6","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-implants#example","content":"Below is a simplified example of talking to NukeRat's implant API. The original sample lives in the nukeratframework/api repository."},"1835":{"id":"/docs/custom-implants-7","page_id":"/docs/custom-implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/custom-implants#custom-channel","content":"If you want a communication path that is not the default HTTP channel, you will need a custom channel setup. For that, read Custom channels to see how it works."},"1836":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"page","content":"Fast setup","url":"/docs/fast-setup"},"1837":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-0","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#install-the-server","content":"Install the server"},"1838":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-1","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#create-an-operator-key","content":"Create an operator key"},"1839":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-2","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#start-the-server","content":"Start the server"},"1840":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-3","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#open-the-client","content":"Open the client"},"1841":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-4","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#configure-a-server","content":"Configure a server"},"1842":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-5","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#configure-a-listener","content":"Configure a listener"},"1843":{"id":"/docs/fast-setup-6","page_id":"/docs/fast-setup","type":"heading","url":"/docs/fast-setup#build-an-implant","content":"Build an implant"},"184