pe":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#how-to-use","content":"To use the AI Agent tab, follow these steps:"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-11","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#configure-an-agent","content":"First, configure an agent in NukeRat > Agents, then click New. For more details about agents, see the AI agents section."},"1889":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-12","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#select-an-agent","content":"After opening the AI Agent tab, use the model selector next to Settings and choose a configured agent."},"1890":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-13","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#send-a-message","content":"If everything is configured correctly, you can send a message to the agent."},"1891":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-14","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"Some NukeRat client tabs expose Copy context in the panel right click menu. It uses the  icon and copies the current tab payload to your clipboard as JSON."},"1892":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-15","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"In other words, context here means the current content of that tab."},"1893":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-16","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"You can paste that content into your message with Ctrl+V without hurting readability. Copy context is available in these tabs:"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-17","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"Terminal"},"1895":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-18","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"File Browser"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-19","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"Processes"},"1897":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-20","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#about-tab-context","content":"Sessions"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-21","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#settings","content":"Each open AI Agent tab has its own settings. Click Settings in the tab header to open them."},"1899":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-22","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#system-prompt","content":"The system prompt defines the system message sent with every request in that tab. It does not affect other AI Agent tabs."},"1900":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-23","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#system-prompt","content":"Changing the system prompt is useful for adjusting agent behavior, such as language, response length, and tone."},"1901":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-24","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#system-prompt","content":"One of the main advantages is jailbreaking restrictive models such as ChatGPT."},"1902":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-25","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#context-configuration","content":"In simple terms, context is the history attached to every message in that tab, even if you clear the chat history."},"1903":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-26","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#context-configuration","content":"Context can be used for different purposes, such as moving memory from one tab to another without resending previous messages."},"1904":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-27","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#context-configuration","content":"This feature can also help with jailbreaks, since some models become less restrictive based on conversation history."},"1905":{"id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent-28","page_id":"/docs/interface/ai-agent","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/ai-agent#context-configuration","content":"Below is a simple context example:"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"page","content":"Attack graph","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-0","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#session-information","content":"Session information"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-1","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#relay-connections","content":"Relay connections"},"1909":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-2","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","content":"The attack graph tab is a graphical view of connected and active sessions. Its main purpose is to help visualize network pivoting and relay paths."},"1910":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-3","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","content":"You can open the Attack Graph tab from the  icon in the top bar, or from Tabs > Attack Graph."},"1911":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-4","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","content":"When you open the tab, the client sends a request to every configured team server and builds a separate graph for each one."},"1912":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-5","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","content":"The graphs update automatically when a session connects or when privilege level changes, for example from user to admin."},"1913":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-6","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#session-information","content":"Each session is shown as a monitor icon with the operating system image inside. If the session already has elevated privileges, the monitor appears in red."},"1914":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-7","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#session-information","content":"You can also double click a session to open a side panel with more information."},"1915":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-8","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#relay-connections","content":"When two sessions are connected through a relay, an arrow is drawn between them with a label showing the relay type, either TCP or SMB."},"1916":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-9","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#relay-connections","content":"In some cases, a session will disappear from the graph view. This can happen for two reasons:"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-10","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#relay-connections","content":"The parent session no longer has an active relay"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs-11","page_id":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/attack-graphs#relay-connections","content":"The child session connected without using a relay"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"page","content":"Credentials","url":"/docs/interface/credentials"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-0","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#how-to-use","content":"How to use"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-1","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#add-a-credential","content":"Add a credential"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-2","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Fill in the fields"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-3","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#access-the-credential","content":"Access the credential"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-4","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials","content":"The credentials tab serves two main purposes:"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-5","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials","content":"Share credentials between operators more easily"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-6","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials","content":"Attach them automatically to an operation report"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-7","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials","content":"You can open the Credentials tab from the  icon in the top bar, or from Tabs > Credentials."},"1928":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-8","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#how-to-use","content":"To use the credentials tab, follow these steps:"},"1929":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-9","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#add-a-credential","content":"Click the Add button to add a new credential."},"1930":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-10","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Each credential is tied to a host and a team server, so both must be configured beforehand."},"1931":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-11","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#access-the-credential","content":"After adding it, double click the credential row to open the side panel and view the stored credential text."},"1932":{"id":"/docs/interface/credentials-12","page_id":"/docs/interface/credentials","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/credentials#access-the-credential","content":"Note that the credentials list does not require selecting a host or server. When you open the tab, the client already sends a request to every configured team server."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"page","content":"File server","url":"/docs/interface/file-server"},"1934":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-0","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#how-to-use","content":"How to use"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-1","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#create-a-file-server-listener","content":"Create a file server listener"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-2","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#select-a-file-server","content":"Select a file server"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-3","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#upload-a-file","content":"Upload a file"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-4","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#extra-tip","content":"Extra tip"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-5","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server","content":"The file server tab lets you manage file servers already configured in NukeRat. It helps you host files quickly and efficiently, for example payloads."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-6","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server","content":"You can open the File Server tab from the  icon in the top bar, or from Tabs > File Server."},"1941":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-7","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#how-to-use","content":"To use the file server tab, follow these steps:"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-8","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#create-a-file-server-listener","content":"First, create a listener of type File server. For more details, see the Listeners section."},"1943":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-9","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#select-a-file-server","content":"At the top of the tab, open the server selector and choose the file server listener you want to manage."},"1944":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-10","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#upload-a-file","content":"Finally, upload the file you want to host."},"1945":{"id":"/docs/interface/file-server-11","page_id":"/docs/interface/file-server","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/file-server#extra-tip","content":"NukeRat file servers are not limited to hosting files. They can also be used to host applications for social engineering, such as fake login pages."},"1946":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"page","content":"File browser","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-0","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#file-download","content":"File download"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-1","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-2","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#windows","content":"Windows"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-3","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#linux","content":"Linux"},"1951":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-4","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#macos","content":"MacOS"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-5","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","content":"The file browser lets you browse files and directories on a host without relying on terminal commands."},"1953":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-6","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","content":"You can open the File Browser tab from the  icon in the top bar, or from Tabs > File Browser."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-7","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","content":"Before using the file browser, select the target host by clicking Host next to the tab name."},"1955":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-8","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","content":"Once you select a host, the client requests a directory listing for that host. The response populates the file browser on first load."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-9","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","content":"The file and directory tree on the left lets you navigate folders and files on the selected host more quickly, while the search bar\nabove the tree filters the tree locally. It does not send new listing commands to the host."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-10","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#file-download","content":"Click a file to view its raw contents. Use the Download button to save it locally."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-11","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#file-download","content":"Image files are displayed inline in the file browser."},"1959":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-12","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#file-download","content":"When a file is larger than 25 MB, the file browser shows a warning instead of loading the full content. Download the file from the terminal if you need the full payload."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-13","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#how-it-works","content":"For directory listing, the implant reads the directory and converts the result into a custom JSON format for the client to render. For file downloads, the implant returns the raw file bytes."},"1961":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-14","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#windows","content":"Uses kernel32.dll (FindFirstFileW, FindNextFileW, GetLogicalDriveStringsW, GetFileAttributesW). The core logic is in implants/windows/stageless/capabilities/folders.nim."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-15","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#linux","content":"Uses POSIX opendir, readdir, and stat. The core logic is in implants/linux/stageless/capabilities/folders.nim."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer-16","page_id":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/folder-explorer#macos","content":"Uses the macapi module (ListDirEntries, PathExists, PathIsDir). The core logic is in implants/mac/stageless/capabilities/folders.go."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"page","content":"Sessions","url":"/docs/interface/hosts"},"1965":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-0","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/hosts#session-information","content":"Session information"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-1","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"heading","url":"/docs/interface/hosts#removing-a-session","content":"Removing a session"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-2","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/hosts","content":"The sessions tab is the main way to monitor sessions connected to every configured team server."},"1968":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-3","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/hosts","content":"You can open the Sessions tab from the  icon in the top bar, or from Tabs > Sessions."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-4","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/hosts","content":"When you open the tab, sessions from every configured team server are listed automatically, so you do not need to select a team server."},"1970":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-5","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/hosts","content":"To search more easily, use the Filter sessions... field at the top of the tab to filter by any string in the session information."},"1971":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-6","page_id":"/docs/interface/hosts","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/hosts#session-information","content":"To see all session information, double click the session row to open a side panel with more details."},"1972":{"id":"/docs/interface/hosts-7","page_id":