url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"Start the background keylogger"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-82","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"keylogger stop"},"2117":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-83","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"Stop the background keylogger"},"2118":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-84","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"screenshot"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-85","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"Capture a single screenshot"},"2120":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-86","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"screenshot start"},"2121":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-87","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"Start the screenshot capture loop"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-88","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"screenshot stop"},"2123":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-89","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"Stop the screenshot capture loop"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-90","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"armory"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/interface/terminal-91","page_id":"/docs/interface/terminal","type":"text","url":"/docs/interface/terminal#commands","content":"List available armory tools"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/introduction","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","url":"/docs/introduction"},"2127":{"id":"/docs/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"heading","url":"/docs/introduction#about","content":"About"},"2128":{"id":"/docs/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"heading","url":"/docs/introduction#purpose","content":"Purpose"},"2129":{"id":"/docs/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"heading","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Features"},"2130":{"id":"/docs/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"heading","url":"/docs/introduction#give-it-a-spin","content":"Give it a spin"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#about","content":"NukeRat is a post exploitation framework focused on red team campaigns. For comparison, it sits in the same category as Cobalt Strike, Havoc, and similar tools."},"2132":{"id":"/docs/introduction-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#purpose","content":"NukeRat came from one red teamer's hands on experience with other frameworks on the market. Yes, we already have strong frameworks out there, including open source projects, but each one still has limits that can get in the way day to day."},"2133":{"id":"/docs/introduction-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#purpose","content":"So NukeRat was built to fill gaps left by some of what we have today, and at the same time bring a few things that feel different."},"2134":{"id":"/docs/introduction-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#purpose","content":"All of that while keeping things simple, with \"Make it simple\" as a core motto."},"2135":{"id":"/docs/introduction-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"A quick summary of what NukeRat offers:"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/introduction-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Armory: A collection of 543 ready to use utilities (BOFs and .NET tools) you can pull from the official armory repository."},"2137":{"id":"/docs/introduction-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Evasion: Windows implants ship with essential techniques for modern environments built in, such as indirect syscalls, sleep masks, string obfuscation, and more."},"2138":{"id":"/docs/introduction-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Cross platform: Out of the box, NukeRat supports Linux and macOS, including ARM based systems."},"2139":{"id":"/docs/introduction-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Custom channels: HTTP and HTTPS channels with custom body templates, plus fully custom channels for third party platforms like Telegram, Dropbox, and similar services."},"2140":{"id":"/docs/introduction-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"Client interface: Often overlooked, NukeRat's client interface was carefully designed to be easy to use and comfortable for long sessions."},"2141":{"id":"/docs/introduction-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#features","content":"AI Agent: Integrate AI models directly into NukeRat, including local models and custom HTTP providers."},"2142":{"id":"/docs/introduction-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction","type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction#give-it-a-spin","content":"Reading about it only gets you so far. Spin it up, get a session going, and you'll know pretty fast if it's your thing."},"2143":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"page","content":"Channel","url":"/docs/listeners/channel"},"2144":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-0","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"HTTP/HTTPS channel"},"2145":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-1","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"Custom channel"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-2","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#what-about-other-channel-types","content":"What about other channel types?"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-3","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#creating-an-http-channel","content":"Creating an HTTP channel"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-4","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#open-the-listeners-tab","content":"Open the listeners tab"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-5","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Fill in the fields"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-6","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#click-create","content":"Click Create"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-7","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"HTTPS and certificates"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-8","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#advanced-customizations","content":"Advanced customizations"},"2153":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-9","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"Custom body"},"2154":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-10","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#notes","content":"Notes"},"2155":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-11","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel","content":"For an implant to communicate with the team server, you need a channel already configured for it."},"2156":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-12","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel","content":"Currently, there are two channel categories:"},"2157":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-13","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel","content":"HTTP/HTTPS"},"2158":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-14","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel","content":"Custom"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-15","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"This is the simplest and most common channel to set up. Beyond a URL for the implant to reach, it does not require any extra configuration."},"2160":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-16","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"When creating this channel, you can also tweak more advanced settings, such as:"},"2161":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-17","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"Changing the URL path (example: https://example.com/path -> https://example.com/path/custom)"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-18","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"Response headers"},"2163":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-19","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#httphttps-channel","content":"Custom body templates for implant requests and listener responses"},"2164":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-20","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"This is the most complex channel type, but also the most flexible. A custom channel is essential when you need highly specific communication, for example:"},"2165":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-21","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"Telegram"},"2166":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-22","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"Discord"},"2167":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-23","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"Dropbox"},"2168":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-24","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"It has no presets, so you will need to configure everything manually. That includes the implant creation step, where you must write a custom code snippet for communication with the channel."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-25","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-channel","content":"You will not find a \"custom channel\" option in the creation interface, since it has to be set up manually. To learn more, see Custom C2."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-26","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#what-about-other-channel-types","content":"Some frameworks still support alternative channels like DNS, DoH, mTLS, and others. We chose these two channel types specifically to match current trends, especially the demand for integration with external communication services like Telegram, Google Drive, and so on."},"2171":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-27","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#what-about-other-channel-types","content":"There are no plans to officially add more channel types in the future."},"2172":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-28","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#what-about-other-channel-types","content":"If you still need a specific channel type, such as DNS, you will need to use Custom Channel to build that communication yourself."},"2173":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-29","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#creating-an-http-channel","content":"The HTTP channel (without SSL) is the simplest one to create. Follow the steps below:"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-30","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#open-the-listeners-tab","content":"Open NukeRat > Listeners > Add."},"2175":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-31","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Name: Choose a name so you can recognize the listener easily."},"2176":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-32","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Type: Select Channel"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-33","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Channel: Choose http or https"},"2178":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-34","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Server: Choose the team server where the listener will be created (must be configured beforehand)"},"2179":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-35","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#fill-in-the-fields","content":"Port to open: The port that will be used"},"2180":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-36","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#click-create","content":"If everything is correct, the listener will be created and listed in the listeners tab."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-37","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#click-create","content":"Once you finish creating it, you can use the implant to communicate with the channel you just set up."},"2182":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-38","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#click-create","content":"The HTTP channel (without SSL) encrypts implant requests and listener responses with the shared AES 256 key from implant build time. Even if someone captures the HTTP session, the bodies stay unreadable without that key. See Communication encryption for how payloads look on the wire."},"2183":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-39","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"To use SSL, choose https in the Channel field when you create the listener. A Certificate section appears below the port field. Under Source, pick self signed or manual."},"2184":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-40","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"Self signed certificates are generated automatically by NukeRat using openssl, and you can customize the generation settings, including days, CN, SAN, key type, and more."},"2185":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-41","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"Some of these fields show example values in the placeholder. Obviously, you should not use those values exactly, as that can lead to detection through certificate signature matching."},"2186":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-42","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"For manual certificates, paste or upload your certificate and private key PEM (for example from a provider such as Namecheap)."},"2187":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-43","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#https-and-certificates","content":"Like the HTTP channel (without SSL), the HTTPS channel uses the same AES 256 encryption for implant bodies. TLS protects the transport, and the bodies stay encrypted even under SSL/TLS inspection."},"2188":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-44","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#advanced-customizations","content":"HTTP/HTTPS channels include a few fields under Advanced:"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-45","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#advanced-customizations","content":"Endpoint path: The URL path the implant will use to communicate"},"2190":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-46","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#advanced-customizations","content":"Response headers: Response headers for the channel"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-47","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#advanced-customizations","content":"Custom body: Preset (off, custom, or the built in json and xml examples) plus optional Listener response and Implant request templates when custom body is not off"},"2192":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-48","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"By default, communication between the implant and the server is straightforward: a string is encrypted and attached to the body, for example:"},"2193":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-49","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"Notice that the body is just a blob of encrypted data, with no specific structure. With a custom body, you can define a shared structure between implant and server, for example:"},"2194":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-50","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"Server response:"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-51","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"Implant request:"},"2196":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-52","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"In the example above, once configured, the channel will send responses in the defined format and expect the same format in implant requests."},"2197":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-53","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#custom-body","content":"We added some custom body examples in the channel creation interface itself, but it is not recommended to use them exactly as shown, since they can trigger detection through body signature matching."},"2198":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-54","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#notes","content":"For the implant to communicate correctly, replicate the same Listener response and Implant request templates during implant creation. The listener reads implantBody and wraps replies with listenerResponse at runtime (see Implant listener API)."},"2199":{"id":"/docs/listeners/channel-55","page_id":"/docs/listeners/channel","type":"text","url":"/docs/listeners/channel#notes","content":"On HTTP/HTTPS channels, when implantBody is empty or exactly §content§, the entire raw request body is encrypted. With a custom structure, only the §content§ slice is encrypted. The listener extracts that slice before decryption."},"2200":{"id":"/docs/listeners/file-server","page_id":"/docs/listeners/file-server","type":"page","content":"File server","url":"/docs/listeners/file-server"},"2201":{"id":"/docs/listeners/file-server-0","page_id":"/docs/listeners/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/file-server#creating-a-file-server","content":"Creating a file server"},"2202":{"id":"/docs/listeners/file-server-1","page_id":"/docs/listeners/file-server","type":"heading","url":"/docs/listeners/file-server#open-the-listeners-tab","content":"Open the listeners tab"},"2203":{"id":"/docs/listeners/file-server-2","page_id":"/docs/listeners/file-serv