ivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"Parameters:"},"2525":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-22","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--listen / -l: port the implant opens and listens on (also used as the default relay id)"},"2526":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-23","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--target / -t: downstream endpoint as implant://host:port or server://host:port"},"2527":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-24","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--channel / -c: tcp or smb"},"2528":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-25","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--pipe: SMB named pipe. Use a short name such as test or a full \\\\pipe\\\\name path on Windows"},"2529":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-26","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--tls: when the target uses server://, POST to the team server over HTTPS instead of HTTP"},"2530":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-27","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#set-up-the-relay-on-host-a","content":"--id: relay id for relay delete or to override the default listen port id"},"2531":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-28","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#manage-relays","content":"Run relay with no arguments to list active relays (listen port, target, mode, status)."},"2532":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-29","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#manage-relays","content":"Stop a relay with:"},"2533":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-30","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#test-the-relay","content":"After that, implant B should be fully operational."},"2534":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-31","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#target-prefix","content":"The target parameter always takes an implant:// or server:// prefix. That tells the implant when to convert traffic to HTTP so it can reach the team server."},"2535":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-32","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#target-prefix","content":"Which one to use is simple. If the next hop is the team server, use server://, that is your exit point. If you are passing traffic to another implant, use implant://."},"2536":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-33","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#cumulative-delay","content":"Relays always forward traffic respecting each implant's configured delay. If traffic hops through several hosts, those delays stack and response times can get noticeably slower."},"2537":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-34","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#how-it-works","content":"Once a relay is created, it starts listening on the configured port."},"2538":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-35","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#how-it-works","content":"When traffic comes in, the implant decrypts the payload, appends its own host tag to the chain, encrypts again, and forwards it to the target."},"2539":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-36","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#how-it-works","content":"The forwarded JSON follows this structure:"},"2540":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-37","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#how-it-works","content":"When the target is another implant, the payload is forwarded as is. When the target is the team server, the implant converts the traffic to HTTP and sends it upstream."},"2541":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-38","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#communication-encryption","content":"All relay traffic is encrypted with a master AES key embedded in each implant at compile time. All implants built for the same team server share the same key."},"2542":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-39","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#communication-encryption","content":"This way implants do not need a prior handshake just to exchange keys, which also reduces that kind of traffic signature."},"2543":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-40","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#communication-encryption","content":"To change the master AES key, edit server.conf under the [implants] section and set relay_aes_key. The value must decode to at least 32 bytes (base64 or hexadecimal). Rebuild implants after changing it."},"2544":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-41","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#communication-encryption","content":"Note: changing the master AES key will break communication with any implant still using the old key."},"2545":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-42","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#windows","content":"TCP uses WSAStartup, socket, bind, listen, accept, connect, send, recv, closesocket, getaddrinfo and freeaddrinfo from ws2_32.dll."},"2546":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-43","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#windows","content":"SMB uses CreateNamedPipeA, ConnectNamedPipe, CreateFileA, WaitNamedPipeA, PeekNamedPipe, ReadFile, WriteFile, DisconnectNamedPipe and CloseHandle from kernel32.dll."},"2547":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-44","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#linux","content":"Uses POSIX sockets (sys/socket.h) with socket, bind, listen, accept, connect, send, recv, setsockopt and getaddrinfo / freeaddrinfo. SMB is not supported."},"2548":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/relay-45","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/relay","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/relay#macos","content":"Uses net.Listen, net.Accept, net.Dial and io.Copy from the Go stdlib. SMB is not supported."},"2549":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"page","content":"SOCKS","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks"},"2550":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-0","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"heading","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#how-to-configure","content":"How to configure"},"2551":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-1","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"heading","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#open-the-network-window","content":"Open the Network window"},"2552":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-2","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"heading","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Configure the proxy"},"2553":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-3","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks","content":"NukeRat has native support for SOCKS, so you can route your traffic through a specific host. The supported version is SOCKS5."},"2554":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-4","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks","content":"The team server opens the listener port. Traffic is relayed through the implant you select as the exit point."},"2555":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-5","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#how-to-configure","content":"To create a SOCKS5 proxy, follow the steps below:"},"2556":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-6","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#open-the-network-window","content":"Go to NukeRat > Network, then click Add."},"2557":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-7","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Team server: listener API endpoint (host and port at the top of the form)"},"2558":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-8","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Type: socks"},"2559":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-9","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Host tag: implant that will proxy outbound connections"},"2560":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-10","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Port target: port opened on the team server for the SOCKS listener"},"2561":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-11","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"User / Password: optional credentials for operator proxy authentication"},"2562":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-12","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Click Create when the fields are set."},"2563":{"id":"/docs/pivoting/socks-13","page_id":"/docs/pivoting/socks","type":"text","url":"/docs/pivoting/socks#configure-the-proxy","content":"Once done, your proxy should appear in the Network table. Point your tools at socks5://<team_server_host>:<port_target>/, including the user and password in the URI when you configured auth."},"2564":{"id":"/docs/reports","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"page","content":"Reports","url":"/docs/reports"},"2565":{"id":"/docs/reports-0","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"heading","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"Activity report"},"2566":{"id":"/docs/reports-1","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"heading","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"Hosts report"},"2567":{"id":"/docs/reports-2","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"heading","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Indicators of Compromise"},"2568":{"id":"/docs/reports-3","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"heading","url":"/docs/reports#example-of-report","content":"Example of report"},"2569":{"id