","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"Up to 200 terminal commands (time, hostname, user, command)"},"2582":{"id":"/docs/reports-17","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"Captured credentials"},"2583":{"id":"/docs/reports-18","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"Up to 200 captured credentials"},"2584":{"id":"/docs/reports-19","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"When you create an activity report, you can enable Hide credentials to mask credential contents in the export."},"2585":{"id":"/docs/reports-20","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#activity-report","content":"Tip: queue activity rows come from terminal queue entries. You can remove commands from the queue by going to NukeRat > Queue. That lets you drop unwanted captures before you generate the report."},"2586":{"id":"/docs/reports-21","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"This is the simplest report. It lists up to 200 compromised hosts in a single table with:"},"2587":{"id":"/docs/reports-22","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"Hostname"},"2588":{"id":"/docs/reports-23","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"User"},"2589":{"id":"/docs/reports-24","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"Operating system"},"2590":{"id":"/docs/reports-25","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"Internal IP"},"2591":{"id":"/docs/reports-26","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"External IP"},"2592":{"id":"/docs/reports-27","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#hosts-report","content":"PID of the NukeRat implant"},"2593":{"id":"/docs/reports-28","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"The IOC report is the most complex of the three. It breaks down every IOC stored for the operation across these sections:"},"2594":{"id":"/docs/reports-29","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Section"},"2595":{"id":"/docs/reports-30","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Description"},"2596":{"id":"/docs/reports-31","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Target scope"},"2597":{"id":"/docs/reports-32","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"List of compromised hosts"},"2598":{"id":"/docs/reports-33","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"IP Addresses"},"2599":{"id":"/docs/reports-34","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"List of IP addresses"},"2600":{"id":"/docs/reports-35","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Domains"},"2601":{"id":"/docs/reports-36","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"List of domains"},"2602":{"id":"/docs/reports-37","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Files"},"2603":{"id":"/docs/reports-38","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"List of file paths detected from command output"},"2604":{"id":"/docs/reports-39","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Hashes"},"2605":{"id":"/docs/reports-40","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"List of file hashes (MD5, SHA1 and SHA256)"},"2606":{"id":"/docs/reports-41","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"NukeRat fills the IOC database automatically. Terminal commands are scanned with regex rules for IPv4 addresses, domains, and known file extensions. Files transferred through the file server also get MD5, SHA1, and SHA256 hashes recorded. Nothing dynamic runs behind the scenes, like an AI model processing the output. So you may get mismatched indicators or plain capture errors, though that is not very common."},"2607":{"id":"/docs/reports-42","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#indicators-of-compromise","content":"Tip: to address any indicator capture issues, NukeRat has an IOC editor where you can review captured indicators and remove them. Open it from NukeRat > IOCs."},"2608":{"id":"/docs/reports-43","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#example-of-report","content":"Below is an example of what the report styling looks like:"},"2609":{"id":"/docs/reports-44","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#example-of-report","content":"Careful opening the report. You might get hit with a visual flashbang because it's in white mode ¯\\(ツ)/¯"},"2610":{"id":"/docs/reports-45","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#limitations","content":"All reports are generated on the team server you select. That means each report is limited to the hosts connected to that specific server."},"2611":{"id":"/docs/reports-46","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#limitations","content":"If you are working with more than one team server, you will need to generate a report for each server separately."},"2612":{"id":"/docs/reports-47","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#feedbacks","content":"Report generation is still experimental. Expect it to change more often than most other NukeRat features. Mostly because we are still\nfiguring out what operators actually need from reports."},"2613":{"id":"/docs/reports-48","page_id":"/docs/reports","type":"text","url":"/docs/reports#feedbacks","content":"If you have feedback or suggestions, open an issue on GitHub."},"2614":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"page","content":"Implants","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants"},"2615":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-0","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Communication encryption"},"2616":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-1","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#request-body-template-implantbody","content":"Request body template (implantBody)"},"2617":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-2","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#response-body-template-listenerresponse","content":"Response body template (listenerResponse)"},"2618":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-3","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"Silent HTTP 200 (empty body)"},"2619":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-4","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#host-validation","content":"Host validation"},"2620":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-5","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"heading","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"Main endpoint"},"2621":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-6","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants","content":"Implant API exposed through the HTTP/HTTPS listener process. This is not the team server management REST API used by the client and automation."},"2622":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-7","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants","content":"The implant uses one path per channel: endpointPath from the listener profile (normalized by the server to start with /, with no trailing slash except /). GET and POST are registered on that path."},"2623":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-8","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants","content":"We recommend reading the advanced guide on Custom Implants, which walks through the implant API. That can save you a lot of trouble."},"2624":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-9","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Every request from the implant must be encrypted. This is not optional. The team server always expects an encrypted body."},"2625":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-10","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Encryption uses an AES-256 key shared by the implant and the team server. The key is created when the implant is built and stored in the database so the team server can decrypt incoming requests."},"2626":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-11","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Important: every implant request body must contain two parts:"},"2627":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-12","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Challenge - 9 random bytes encrypted with the AES-256 key (always exactly 9 bytes)"},"2628":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-13","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"Payload - the request JSON encrypted with the same AES-256 key"},"2629":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-14","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"The two blobs are concatenated and sent together. For example:"},"2630":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-15","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"abcdef is the challenge"},"2631":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-16","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"{\"output\": \"123\"} is the payload"},"2632":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-17","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"The encrypted output would be:"},"2633":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-18","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"The challenge exists so the listener can find the right key to decrypt the payload. As noted above, the challenge is always 9 bytes. That keeps validation fast without touching the real payload, which is often much larger."},"2634":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-19","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"If no key works, the listener returns HTTP 200 with an empty body."},"2635":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-20","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"On the wire, each encrypted segment is a 12-byte nonce followed by AES-GCM ciphertext including the 16-byte auth tag. The listener treats the first 37 bytes (12 + 9 + 16) as the probe blob and decrypts the remainder as the payload blob. Both must decrypt with the same key row from the AesKeys table."},"2636":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-21","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#communication-encryption","content":"The minimum POST body size is 65 bytes (probe segment plus at least one payload byte). Shorter bodies get HTTP 200 with an empty response."},"2637":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-22","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#request-body-template-implantbody","content":"When the channel sets implantBody (or legacy beaconBody), the raw HTTP body must match that template. The marker §content§ is where the encrypted bytes belong. The listener extracts the substring matched at §content§ and only that slice is decrypted."},"2638":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-23","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#request-body-template-implantbody","content":"If implantBody is empty or exactly §content§, the entire raw body is decrypted."},"2639":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-24","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#request-body-template-implantbody","content":"If a non-empty template is configured and the body does not match, the listener returns HTTP 200 with an empty body (no decryption)."},"2640":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-25","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#response-body-template-listenerresponse","content":"Handler JSON is encrypted into a single blob the same way as implant requests. By default that blob is the entire response body. When listenerResponse is set, the encrypted bytes replace §content§ in that template."},"2641":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-26","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#response-body-template-listenerresponse","content":"Successful encrypted responses use Content-Type: application/octet-stream. The HTTP status is 200 unless noted below."},"2642":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-27","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"These cases return HTTP 200 with an empty body and no encrypted JSON:"},"2643":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-28","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"GET on the listener path"},"2644":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-29","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"POST with an empty body"},"2645":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-30","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"Body shorter than the minimum wire length"},"2646":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-31","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"No database AES key decrypts probe + payload"},"2647":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-32","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"implantBody template mismatch"},"2648":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-33","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"JSON parse error after decryption"},"2649":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-34","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#silent-http-200-empty-body","content":"Implant payload validation error (option missing/invalid, required fields, and so on)"},"2650":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-35","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#host-validation","content":"After decryption, if the parsed payload is a single JSON object with a host field, the listener checks that tag exists in the host registry. If not, it responds with HTTP 400 and encrypted JSON:"},"2651":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-36","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#host-validation","content":"JSON arrays (batch output) skip this pre-check. last_seen is still updated from the first array element when it includes host."},"2652":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-37","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"This is the main listener path (endpointPath). GET always returns HTTP 200 with an empty body. POST carries encrypted implant traffic as described above."},"2653":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-38","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"Processing order on POST: read raw body → optional implantBody extraction → decrypt → JSON parse → parse_implant_payload → optional host registry check → handler (main_core → main_actions)."},"2654":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-39","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"Requests fall into 3 main modes:"},"2655":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-40","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"new sends the fingerprint and registers the host on first contact"},"2656":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-41","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"input polls the queue for pending commands"},"2657":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-42","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"output sends command results"},"2658":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-43","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"There are also optional auxiliary modes:"},"2659":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-44","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"kill_task cancels a queue task"},"2660":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-45","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","type":"text","url":"/docs/rest-api/implants#main-endpoint","content":"error reports a task failure without option"},"2661":{"id":"/docs/rest-api/implants-46","page_id":"/docs/rest-api/implants","